@meta
id: 09-02
title: Delete to either end of the line
chapter: 09
type: lesson
skills: op-motion
par: 17
minutes: 4
vscode: click, then Shift-End or Shift-Home, then Delete
open: release-notes.md

@brief
Two lines of a release note need trimming. One has a marker left at the
front that should not ship. The other has a note stuck on the end that
should not ship either.

    refunds run nightly at 02:00 UTC
    The thirty day window is configurable.

Leave the two sentences and nothing else, then save.

@keys
| `$` | to the end of the line |
| `0` | to the very start of the line |
| `d$` | delete from the cursor to the end of the line |
| `d0` | delete from the start of the line up to the cursor |

@hints
Both edits are the same shape: park the cursor on the first character you
want to keep, or the first one you want to lose, and let a line motion
say where to stop.
--
`d$` clears everything from the cursor rightwards. `d0` clears everything
leftwards, back to column one. The character under the cursor survives
`d0` and dies with `d$`.
--
On the first line, land on `refunds` and press `d0`. On the second,
`f` onto the first `X` and press `d$`.

@solution
    2w d0    j fX d$    :wq<Enter>

`d$` has a shorthand you already met in chapter 8: `D`. They are the same
edit, and `D` is one key rather than two. `d0` has no shorthand, which
tells you something about how often each gets used.

Two details worth having straight.

`d$` is inclusive of the character under the cursor — it runs to the last
character of the line and takes it. `d0` is exclusive at the cursor end:
it deletes everything to the left and leaves the character you are sitting
on. That asymmetry is not arbitrary. `$` is an inclusive motion and `0` is
an exclusive one, and the operator simply obeys whichever motion you gave
it. Every operator in vim inherits that from its motion.

The leftover space after `d$` does not matter here — the grader ignores
trailing whitespace, as does every sane linter. If you want it gone
anyway, land one character to the left before deleting.

@start release-notes.md
FIXME: refunds run nightly at 02:00 UTC
The thirty day window is configurable. XXX check with finance

@expected release-notes.md
refunds run nightly at 02:00 UTC
The thirty day window is configurable.
